        {
          "id": "spf:tech-fleet-sprint-demo-slide-deck-template",
          "label": "Tech Fleet Sprint Demo Slide Deck Template"
        }
      ],
      "What Mindset Do You Have When You Practice This Practice?": "Psychological Safety comes from believing that everyone belongs and has something worth hearing, even the newest person in the room. You believe people learn by trying and failing, that fresh contributions are welcome no matter someone's experience, and that respectfully challenging the way things work makes the work better.",
      "What Habits Do You Hold When You Practice This Practice?": "In practice, this looks like pausing so quieter voices can speak, listening fully before responding, and sharing your own mistakes and uncertainties so others feel safe to do the same. You invite questions, welcome disagreement, and treat every person as a full member of the team.",
      "Practice Description": "Psychological Safety is the shared sense that it is safe to speak up, ask questions, admit mistakes, and challenge ideas without being embarrassed or punished. Research on strong teams, from Amy Edmondson to Google's own studies, points to it as the single biggest factor in how well a team performs. It grows in stages: feeling included, then safe to learn, then safe to contribute, then safe to challenge. When it is present, people bring their whole selves and their best thinking to the work.",
      "Where Can I Learn More?": "Amy Edmondson on psychological safety (Harvard, author of The Fearless Organization): https://amycedmondson.com/psychological-safety/"
    },
    {
      "id": "spf:agility",
      "type": [
        "schema:DefinedTerm",
        "spf:Practice",
        "skos:Concept"
      ],
      "Practice Name": "Agility",
      "Data Type": [
        {
          "id": "spf:practices",
          "label": "Practices"
        }
      ],
      "Difference between Practices and Skills": "Practices:\n1. A practice requires working with other people\n1. A practice never stops being built; you never become an expert in a practice, but the behaviors of a practice become more permanent over time\n1. A practice is rooted in beliefs and behaviors\n1. A practice can be measured through what other people believe about you\n1. A practice cannot be concretely measured through deliverables\n1. A practice is an intentional choice someone makes to do\n1. You learn how to get good about your practice by reflecting to yourself\n1. A practice can be measured through what you believe about yourself\n1. You learn how to get good about your practice by reflecting with others\nSkills:\n1. A skill enables you to make a deliverable\n1. A skill is measured through how many times you have applied it\n1. A skill is rooted in  knowledge and theory\n1. A skill applies to a specific activity\n1. A skill is expected to be performed in specific job functions\n1. A skill is something someone can become an expert in it with a lot of experience\n1. A skill you can perform yourself\n1. A skill can be practiced by applying it to work you deliver or duties you perform\n1. A skill is required in specific jobs, people may not have a choice to perform the skill if the job requires them\n1. A skill gets practiced by doing work and performing duties\n\nWhat they both have in common: \n1. A practice and a skill can be lost if  not continued to be practiced\n1. A practice and a skill require willingness to perform",
      "Practice Components": [
        {
          "id": "spf:adaptability",
          "label": "Adaptability"
        },
        {
          "id": "spf:interactions-with-individuals",
          "label": "Interactions with Individuals"
        },
        {
          "id": "spf:delivering-working-results",
          "label": "Delivering Working Results"
        },
        {
          "id": "spf:customer-collaboration",
          "label": "Customer Collaboration"
        },
        {
          "id": "spf:failing-fast",
          "label": "Failing Fast"
        }
      ],
      "Practice Level 0 Definition": "1. You are not aware of what makes up the practice\n1. You are not willing to practice it\n1. You tried to practice it, but stopped",
      "Practice Level 1 Definition": "1. You believe it's valuable to practice the practice\n1. You are willing to practice it\n1. You have knowledge about the components of the practice\n1. You've started practicing with others and reflecting",
      "Practice Level 2 Definition": "1. You've seen growth in your own practice through reflection\n1. You are starting to work on changing habits",
      "Practice Level 3 Definition": "1. Your habits are starting to become automatic in your practice with others\n1. You’re so practiced in it, that you are a role model to others in the practice\n1. You can and do advocate for the practice when working with others",
      "Other Practices Required": [
