      ],
      "What Mindset Do You Have When You Practice This Practice?": "This practice rests on the belief that the team's combined judgment usually beats any single person's, and that a decision only holds when people have had a genuine say. You stay open to changing a decision when the situation changes, rather than clinging to your first call.",
      "What Habits Do You Hold When You Practice This Practice?": "In everyday work, this looks like gathering input from the group, checking your assumptions out loud, and giving people time to reflect and respond before you lock anything in. You adjust decisions as you learn, and you steer toward a choice the whole team can get behind.",
      "Practice Description": "Collective Decision Making is how a team makes choices together so people actually support them, instead of one person deciding for everyone. A team that practices it draws on the knowledge already in the room and gives people time to weigh in before committing. It relies on habits like building consensus, checking assumptions, and staying flexible as situations change. Decisions made this way tend to stick, because the people carrying them out helped shape them.",
      "Where Can I Learn More?": "Atlassian Team Playbook, practical plays for healthy teamwork: https://www.atlassian.com/team-playbook"
    },
    {
      "id": "spf:continuous-improvement",
      "type": [
        "schema:DefinedTerm",
        "spf:Practice",
        "skos:Concept"
      ],
      "Practice Name": "Continuous Improvement",
      "Data Type": [
        {
          "id": "spf:practices",
          "label": "Practices"
        }
      ],
      "Difference between Practices and Skills": "Practices:\n1. A practice requires working with other people\n1. A practice never stops being built; you never become an expert in a practice, but the behaviors of a practice become more permanent over time\n1. A practice is rooted in beliefs and behaviors\n1. A practice can be measured through what other people believe about you\n1. A practice cannot be concretely measured through deliverables\n1. A practice is an intentional choice someone makes to do\n1. You learn how to get good about your practice by reflecting to yourself\n1. A practice can be measured through what you believe about yourself\n1. You learn how to get good about your practice by reflecting with others\nSkills:\n1. A skill enables you to make a deliverable\n1. A skill is measured through how many times you have applied it\n1. A skill is rooted in  knowledge and theory\n1. A skill applies to a specific activity\n1. A skill is expected to be performed in specific job functions\n1. A skill is something someone can become an expert in it with a lot of experience\n1. A skill you can perform yourself\n1. A skill can be practiced by applying it to work you deliver or duties you perform\n1. A skill is required in specific jobs, people may not have a choice to perform the skill if the job requires them\n1. A skill gets practiced by doing work and performing duties\n\nWhat they both have in common: \n1. A practice and a skill can be lost if  not continued to be practiced\n1. A practice and a skill require willingness to perform",
